WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.394
A scan becomes a room of walls.

00:00:02.394 --> 00:00:05.371
Then a person and an agent both edit the same wall,

00:00:05.371 --> 00:00:06.453
at the same moment.

00:00:06.453 --> 00:00:06.995
Who wins?

00:00:10.585 --> 00:00:11.554
Hi, I'm Corvina!

00:00:11.554 --> 00:00:15.431
I ran Mutiny BIM, modelled a room shaped like a scan's output,

00:00:15.431 --> 00:00:18.338
through an agent, and raced editors on one wall.

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:20.617
Chapter one.

00:00:20.617 --> 00:00:22.035
A wall is a record.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:28.982
Every element is a plain record.

00:00:28.982 --> 00:00:32.156
A wall is a start point, an end point and a

00:00:32.156 --> 00:00:33.311
height, stored in millimetres.

00:00:33.731 --> 00:00:35.883
It has a thickness and a type.

00:00:35.883 --> 00:00:40.187
Its class, arch dot wall, comes from the architecture add-on, not from the kernel.

00:00:40.607 --> 00:00:43.566
A partition places it by level, zone and discipline.

00:00:43.986 --> 00:00:47.436
And it keeps a version, plus a ledger of which fields

00:00:47.436 --> 00:00:48.690
someone wrote on purpose.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:50.572
That ledger decides every conflict later.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:56.418
A scan pipeline emits geometry, so here that output is a script:

00:00:56.418 --> 00:01:00.285
walls two hundred and thirty millimetres thick, a slab, a door, a

00:01:00.285 --> 00:01:01.574
window and a duct.

00:01:01.994 --> 00:01:04.322
The preview runs it and saves nothing.

00:01:04.322 --> 00:01:08.313
Seven edit steps, with quantities a person can check against the scan.

00:01:08.733 --> 00:01:10.709
Then the server asks a person.

00:01:10.709 --> 00:01:14.001
My first client answered without saying yes, and nothing landed.

00:01:14.421 --> 00:01:18.590
With approval, thirteen records commit as one revision and one undo step,

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:21.022
and every open editor sees them arrive.

00:01:21.442 --> 00:01:25.367
Exported, the room is a valid IFC four point three file of

00:01:25.367 --> 00:01:27.330
two hundred and eighty nine entities.

00:01:28.967 --> 00:01:29.680
Chapter two.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:30.751
The agent's loop.

00:01:34.508 --> 00:01:37.845
The agent writes one script against the kit, and the server checks it.

00:01:38.265 --> 00:01:41.785
Running it gives a preview: what would change, the quantities, and a

00:01:41.785 --> 00:01:42.958
hash of the output.

00:01:42.958 --> 00:01:44.132
Nothing is saved yet.

00:01:44.552 --> 00:01:46.934
A person sees that preview and approves it.

00:01:46.934 --> 00:01:48.721
The agent cannot answer for them.

00:01:49.141 --> 00:01:52.501
Then the server re-runs the script, lands only what matches the

00:01:52.501 --> 00:01:53.723
preview, and commits it.

00:01:55.383 --> 00:01:57.331
That keeps the agent's context small.

00:01:57.331 --> 00:01:58.954
The core lists ten tools.

00:01:58.954 --> 00:02:02.849
With every add-on family on, it lists a hundred and sixty eight.

00:02:04.508 --> 00:02:05.864
Here is that loop landing.

00:02:05.864 --> 00:02:08.034
The first room is already in the plan.

00:02:08.034 --> 00:02:10.475
My client sends a second one, and approves it.

00:02:10.895 --> 00:02:14.161
It appears in an open browser session the moment it commits.

00:02:15.633 --> 00:02:16.325
Chapter three.

00:02:16.325 --> 00:02:17.709
Two editors, one wall.

00:02:21.867 --> 00:02:25.186
Each command tells the server which fields it wrote on purpose.

00:02:25.186 --> 00:02:27.901
A wall move records its start and its end.

00:02:27.901 --> 00:02:30.013
That is the ledger the server checks.

00:02:30.433 --> 00:02:33.544
Two people move the same wall from the same revision.

00:02:33.544 --> 00:02:37.278
The second move is re-run on the new head, writes start differently,

00:02:37.278 --> 00:02:38.834
and is refused by name.

00:02:39.254 --> 00:02:42.310
Now Priya sets the thickness to three hundred millimetres.

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:45.026
The thickness command records no field at all.

00:02:45.446 --> 00:02:49.057
A second person sets two hundred and fifty on the same base.

00:02:49.057 --> 00:02:53.270
There is nothing to compare, so both commit and the later one wins, silently.

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:58.714
So the doc's promise, no last write wins on intent, holds for

00:02:58.714 --> 00:03:00.325
moves, for people and agents.

00:03:00.325 --> 00:03:01.613
For thickness, not yet.

00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:04.097
Chapter four.

00:03:04.097 --> 00:03:05.525
What the IFC carries.

00:03:09.358 --> 00:03:12.627
An open source IFC checker found the room's file valid.

00:03:12.627 --> 00:03:15.570
Reading it entity by entity, the gaps are plain.

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:18.341
Walls and the slab are extruded boxes.

00:03:18.341 --> 00:03:21.363
There are no materials and no common property sets.

00:03:21.783 --> 00:03:24.850
Each wall runs from its start to its end on its centre

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:28.173
line, so every outer corner of the room is missing a small square.

00:03:28.593 --> 00:03:31.628
The room is an IFC space with its net floor area, and

00:03:31.628 --> 00:03:33.146
no shape a viewer can draw.

00:03:33.566 --> 00:03:36.481
And the duct's size lives only in its profile, with no

00:03:36.481 --> 00:03:37.541
property and no flow.

00:03:38.967 --> 00:03:42.967
The maintainers show what a bigger script can make: plans, a single

00:03:42.967 --> 00:03:46.301
line diagram and a switchboard schedule, from one assistant's script.

00:03:46.721 --> 00:03:48.977
That video walks through the finished model.

00:03:48.977 --> 00:03:53.490
In my run, the repository's own examples did the same at a smaller scale.

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:55.666
Chapter five.

00:03:55.666 --> 00:03:56.899
Where a scan fits.

00:04:01.567 --> 00:04:03.107
Mutiny takes no point cloud.

00:04:03.107 --> 00:04:04.955
It is where fitted elements land.

00:04:05.375 --> 00:04:09.730
A fitting stage turns the cloud into wall lines, openings and a floor outline.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:13.881
It writes them as a script, passing every measured dimension, because the

00:04:13.881 --> 00:04:15.124
defaults are in feet.

00:04:15.544 --> 00:04:18.525
A person checks the preview against the scan and approves it.

00:04:18.945 --> 00:04:22.592
The walls land as existing conditions, locked, and design work happens

00:04:22.592 --> 00:04:23.919
on top of them.

00:04:25.433 --> 00:04:29.245
So use Mutiny as the shared model an agent edits through approvals.

00:04:29.245 --> 00:04:31.786
Not yet as the IFC you hand over.

00:04:33.817 --> 00:04:34.562
To recap.

00:04:34.562 --> 00:04:36.796
One script, one preview, one approval.

00:04:36.796 --> 00:04:40.148
Conflicting moves are refused by name, but thickness overwrites.

00:04:40.148 --> 00:04:43.500
And a valid IFC that is thin on data.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.134
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:46.134 --> 00:04:46.767
I'm Corvina.

00:04:46.767 --> 00:04:47.084
Bye!
